## Chemical Science
## 1. Introduction
Amino acids are essential ingredients of biological matter and are crucially involved in a variety of phenomena.1a Sensing of certain amino acids is important in the context of nutritional analysis1b and diagnosis of diseases such as Alzheimer,1c pancreatitis,1d etc. In particular, there is a surge of interest in the sensing of histidine in biological fluids, as it is known to be associated with a variety of functions. Besides being an essential amino acid for human growth,2 histidine is a neurotransmitter and a regulator of metal transmission in mammals.3 The human body uses histidine to manufacture histamine, which is responsible for a wide range of physiological processes.4 Further, abnormal levels of histidine-rich proteins are known to manifest in a variety of ailments such as asthma and advanced liver cirrhosis.5 Amongst the generally employed techniques6 that include colorimetric detection,6a capillary electrophoresis,6b electrochemical methods,6c etc., fluorescence-based sensing of histidine is most attractive due to rapid detection with high sensitivity. In fact, a variety of sensors based on fluorophore-crafted receptors such as metal complexes,7a porphyrin,7b crown ethers,7c peptides,7d polymers,7e etc. have been reported for selective detection of histidine. Although the design of sensors for histidine−one of the most basic amino acids−is simplified by the presence of imidazole ring in the side chain, fluorescencebased sensing by some sensors has been noted to be interfered by other amino acids such as cysteine, tryptophan, tyrosine, lysine, arginine, etc.8 Significantly, chiral fluorescence-based sensors permit enantioselective sensing as well as assay of the optically-enriched forms. Selective sensing of an amino acid and further enantiodiscrimination of the same by fluorescence is challenging, and is heretofore unknown.
Crystalline and porous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with luminescence properties−also termed LMOFs−have emerged as fascinating materials for sensing analytes bound in the pores.10
Luminescence in MOFs may arise as a consequence of one or more of the following: fluorescent organic linkers, luminescent metal ions such as lanthanide ions, combination of both linkers and metal ions, fluorescent guest species that are bound by the MOFs, scintillation, etc.10a LMOFs have been extensively exploited during the last decade for sensing of small molecules, volatile organic compounds (VOCs), explosive nitroaromatic compounds (NACs), odorants, transition metal ions, temperature, pH and biologically-relevant molecules.10 In particular, homochiral MOFs constructed from enantiopure chiral linkers have been demonstrated to be excellent for enantioselective binding and separation of racemic mixtures.11 Although diverse MOFs have been developed for chiral resolution,11 porous homochiral LMOFs have been explored only little, in any, for enantiodifferentiating fluorescence sensing of chiral analytes.12 Herein we report the synthesis and application of a homochiral fluorescent Zn-MOF, i.e., Zn-PLA (Fig. 1)−constructed from a rationally designed fluorescent pyrene-tetralactic acid linker that is endowed inherently with concave features13−for selective sensing as well as enantiodiscrimination of histidine in water.
