## 2.  Results and discussion
## 2.1.  Design and synthesis of chiral pyrene-lactic acid linker $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$
In our comprehensive investigations entailing rational design, synthesis, and demonstration of inclusion chemistry in the solid state, we have shown that the molecular systems such as PTE and PTOH (Fig. 1), which are characterized by orthogonal planes featuring three domains, namely, concave, trough and basin (Fig. 1), exhibit guest inclusion as a consequence of creation of void spaces in the lattices.13 We have further shown that metal-assisted assembly of such molecular systems lead to metal-organic materials that are necessarily porous.13f In our recent investigations, a fluorescent Mn-MOF−constructed from a pyrene-acetic acid, cf. $\mathbf{H_{4}P A A}$ (Fig. 1)−was shown to be porous to permit fluorescence sensing of solvent polarity and explosive nitroaromatics;13f the central pyrene core, which is protected by the orthogonally-oriented aryl rings at the four corners for quenching by the paramagnetic metal ions, is an excellent fluorophore. During these investigations, we discovered that the MOFs constructed with building blocks containing acetic acid moieties at the periphery as in $\mathbf{H_{4}P A A}$ are stable in water. This was indeed the basis for developing a chiral homologous pyrene-tetralactic acid, i.e., $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ , to access homochiral fluorescent MOFs to explore sensing of amino acids in water.
The tetraacid linker $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ was synthesized starting from its precursor tetraphenol PTOH (Fig. 1).13f 4-Fold Mitsunobu reaction of PTOH with $(S)–(-)$ -methyl lactate in the presence of diisopropylazodicarboxylate (DIAD) furnished the chiral tetraester in $74\%$ yield. Hydrolysis of the latter with $\mathrm{K_{2}C O_{3}/M e O H}$ led to the chiral tetralactic acid $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ in a near quantitative yield, cf. ESI.
## 2.2. Synthesis and $\mathbf{X}$ -ray crystal structure determinations of Zn-PLA
The reaction of $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ with $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in $N,N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylformamide- $_\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}O}$ (4:1, $\nu/\nu\big|$ ) solvent mixture in the presence of $45\%$ aqueous $\mathrm{HBF}_{4}$ at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a tightly-capped glass vial led to flaky crystals after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . X-ray single crystal structure determination (deposition number: CCDC 1429649) revealed that the crystals belong to monoclinic system with $\varPi $ space group, cf. ESI. The asymmetric unit cell was found to contain 1.5 molecules of PLA, two $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ metal ions, two coordinated water molecules and one dimethylammonium (DMA) cation. Thus, the molecular formula of the asymmetric repeating unit is $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(L)_{1.5}(H_{2}O)_{2}\bullet M e_{2}N H_{2}]}$ . The 1.5 molecules of PLA account for six units of negative charge, which is compensated by two $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and two DMA cations; unfortunately, one of the DMA cations could not be located due to disorder in the large void volume. A careful analysis showed that one of these DMA cations is, indeed, stabilized inside the voids by strong hydrogen bonds with the oxygen atoms of the carboxylate groups of PLA, cf., ESI. The crystal structure analysis reveals that there exist two crystallographically independent $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions, i.e., Zn1 and Zn2, both of which are tetrahedral in geometry, cf. Fig. 2. Interestingly, Zn1 is found to be coordinated by four oxygen atoms of four ligand carboxylate groups, while $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}2}$ is coordinated by four oxygen atoms of two ligand carboxylates and two water molecules. Thus, Zn1 behaves as a 4-connecting node, while Zn2 serves as a 2-connecting node. In Fig. 2 are shown coordination modes of the carboxylate groups of PLA. The assembly of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and the organic spacer PLA leads to formation of a 3D anionic porous framework structure with channels that propagate down a-axis, Fig. 2. The solvent accessible volume, which includes the missing DMA cation, in Zn-PLA MOF was calculated to be $35.5\%$ by Mercury (gridstep $=0.7\mathrm{\AA}$ and probe radius $=1.2\mathrm{\AA}$ ). Interestingly, the 3D-framework of Zn-PLA corresponds to a new topology with a point symbol of
