$(4.8^{4}.10)_{3}(4^{3}.8^{2}.10)_{2}(8)_{2}$ and 2,4,4,4-c net with stoichiometry $(2\mathrm{-c})_{2}(4\mathrm{-c})_{2}(4\mathrm{-c})_{2}(4\mathrm{-c})_{3}$ , as revealed by TOPOS program. This is based on the consideration of two organic linkers as two 4- connecting units and two different metal ions as 2- and 4-connecting nodes. In Fig. 2 is shown a simplified topological network of the 4-nodal underlying net in standard representation.
The synthesis of Zn-MOF, i.e., Zn-PLA, could be readily adapted to bulk scale synthesis; the MOF synthesized in a large scale was established to be identical to that of the X-ray determined single crystal by comparing the PXRD profile of the pristine Zn-PLA with that simulated for the structure obtained by single crystal X-ray analysis, vide infra. Remarkably, the crystals of Zn
PLA were found to be highly stable when immersed in water for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1d}$ , as revealed by PXRD analysis, vide infra. The TGA of Zn-PLA was found to reveal solvent loss corresponding to ca. $20\%$ up to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ followed by thermal decomposition, cf. ESI.
## 2.3. Fluorescence of chiral Zn-PLA and enantioselective fluorescence sensing of histidine
The crystals of Zn-PLA were found to display brilliant blue emission upon exposure to UV light. In Fig. 3 is shown the solid-state fluorescence spectrum of Zn-PLA with emission maximum at $410\mathrm{nm}$ for excitation at $350\mathrm{nm}$ . The solid-state fluorescence emission spectrum of the precursor organic linker $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ was found to be identical to that of Zn-PLA. This is not surprising given that the orthogonally-oriented aryl rings at 4-corners of the pyrene moiety insulate the fluorescent pyrene core.13f The solid-state fluorescence quantum yields of Zn-PLA and $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ were determined by an integrating sphere setup to be $46\%$ and $28\%$ , respectively, for excitation at $350~\mathrm{nm}$ . The enhancement in the fluorescence quantum yield of Zn-PLA when compared to that of the precursor organic linker $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ should be reconciled from differences in the environments around the pyrene core in the two structures; the conformational rigidity and highly ordered structure of the pyrene fluorophore within the framework presumably contribute to the observed increase in the fluorescence quantum yield of Zn-PLA.14 The latter is indeed a hallmark of MOFs, which deprive the fluorophoric linkers of their torsional freedom. Insofar as the chiroptical properties are concerned, the tetraacid linker $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ was found to display moderate positive specific rotation of $\left[\mathrm{a}\right]^{25}\mathrm{_D}=+15.8^{\mathrm{o}}$ in DMF. The solid-state CD spectra of both $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ and Zn-PLA show similar positive Cotton effects at ca. 280 and $344~\mathrm{nm}$ and negative Cotton effects at ca. 290 and $370\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, cf. Fig. 3. This substantiates the fact that the chirality of the organic linker is preserved in Zn-MOF.
The respectable fluorescence quantum yield, chirality, water stability and significant void volume of ca. $35\%$ were the reasons to explore the utility of Zn-PLA to signal guest binding. To begin with, the fluorescence of the crystals of Zn-PLA suspended as a dispersion in water was examined in the presence of different types of natural L-amino acids that include small (glycine and alanine), nucleophilic (serine and cysteine), hydrophobic (valine, leucine and proline), aromatic (phenylalanine, tyrosine and tryptophan), acidic (aspartic acid) and basic (arginine and histidine) amino acids. Remarkably, a gradual decrease in the fluorescence intensity of the aqueous suspension of Zn-PLA was observed with increasing concentration of L-histidine, while the fluorescence was virtually unaffected by all other amino acids even at concentrations as high as $100~\mathrm{{mM}}$ . For L-histidine employed in $10~\mathrm{mM}$ concentration, the quenching efficiency $(\eta)$ was determined to be $55\%$ ; the quenching efficiency15 is defined as: $\begin{array}{r}{\eta=\left[\frac{I_{0}-I}{I_{0}}\right]\times100\%}\end{array}$ . Further, the competition experiments showed that the fluorescence quenching efficiency of $10~\mathrm{mM}$ Lhistidine in the presence of all other amino acids present in $100~\mathrm{mM}$ concentrations remains almost the same, cf. Fig. 4. Thus, the remarkable selectivity of Zn-MOF to signal binding of Lhistidine over other natural amino acids is clearly evident. The fact that the structural integrity of the crystals of Zn-PLA suspended in water and in the presence of histidine remains intact was established by PXRD analysis, Fig. 4.
Encouraged by the highly selective sensing of histidine by Zn-PLA, its ability to discriminate the enantiomers of histidine was examined. The fluorescence quenching titrations were performed with incremental addition of D- $\cdot(+)$ - and L- $(-)$ -histidine to the dispersion of Zn-PLA in water. The fluorescence quenching titration of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ -PLA with D- $\cdot(+)$ -histidine is shown in Fig. 5. Indeed, the steady-state fluorescence quenching data for both D- $(+)$ and L- $(-)$ -histidine could be readily subjected to linear regression analysis of the Stern-Volmer plots; the Stern-Volmer
## Chemical Science
equation is given by: $\begin{array}{r}{\frac{l_{0}}{l}=1+\mathrm{\delta}K_{\mathrm{SV}}[Q]}\end{array}$ . The Stern-Volmer constants, i.e., $K_{\mathrm{D}}$ and $K_{\mathrm{L}}$ for D- $\cdot(+)$ - and L- $(-)$ -histidine, were determined to be 115 and $64~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ , respectively. Evidently, the D and L isomers of histidine are differentiated by the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF. The quenching of the fluorescence of ZnPLA by D- $(+)$ -histidine is thermodynamically more favorable than that by $\operatorname{L-}(-)$ -histidine. It should be pointed out that the enantiodiscrimination, i.e., $\Delta K=(K_{\mathrm{D}}-K_{\mathrm{L}})$ , of $51~\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ and the enantioselectivity ratio, i.e., $K_{\mathrm{D}}/K_{\mathrm{L}},$ of 1.8 observed for $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ -PLA are akin to those achieved with 'HELIXOL $^{\cdot16}-\mathbf{a}$ well known helicene-based enantioselective fluorescent sensor−for sensing of amines and amino alcohols.
## 2.4. Mechanism of quenching of fluorescence of Zn-PLA by histidine
The emission of Zn-PLA essentially arises from the pyrene core, as is evident from the superimposed solid-state fluorescence spectra of $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ and Zn-PLA shown in Fig. 3. The fact that the fluorescence of aromatic systems such as anthracene and pyrene is quenched by thiols, amines, and aromatic $N.$ -heterocycles such as imidazole by photoinduced electron transfer (PET) mechanism has been well established.17 Given that the amino acids exist in their zwitter ionic forms, it was anticipated a priori that the amino acids such as cysteine, histidine, tryptophan and tyrosine would quench the fluorescence of Zn-PLA. Thus, selective sensing of these amino acids that contain thiol, imidazole, indole and hydroxyphenyl groups was expected. Indeed, we established from independent quenching studies on a model system, i.e., pyrene-tetraether PTE, which is devoid of lactic acid moieties at the periphery, that the fluorescence of the latter is quenched by cysteine, histidine, tryptophan and tyrosine with diffusion limited rate constants, cf. ESI.18 The quenching rate constants determined for PTE are: $k_{\mathrm{q}}(\mathrm{Cys})=3.7\times10^{9}\mathrm{M^{-1}~s^{-1}}$ ; $k_{\mathrm{q}}(\mathrm{His})$ $=3.9\times10^{9}\mathrm{{M^{-1}~s^{-1}};}k_{\mathrm{q}}(\mathrm{{Trp}})=5.1\times10^{9}\mathrm{{M^{-1}~s^{-1}};}k_{\mathrm{q}}(\mathrm{{Tyr}})=6.9\times10^{9}\mathrm{{M^{-1}~s^{-1}}.}$ These rate constants were determined based on steady-state Stern-Volmer quenching constants for quenching of the fluorescence of PTE in DMSO and its singlet lifetime; the latter was determined by nanosecond time-resolved studies to be $18.3~\mathrm{ns}$ , cf. ESI. In light of these results, the observation that the fluorescence of Zn-PLA is not quenched by any of the 20 amino acids other than histidine is remarkable. Most surprising is the fact that the fluorescence of Zn-MOF is not influenced by amino acids such as cysteine, tryptophan and tyrosine. This underscores the unique environment offered by the MOF vis-a-vis PTE; the fluorescence in both cases emanates from the central pyrene core.
The origin of such a remarkable selectively in the quenching by histidine should be reconciled from the attributes of histidine in comparison to other amino acids. Lack of quenching by all amino acids with the exception of histidine suggests that they either cannot quench or those that are capable of quenching cannot establish the required proximity with the pyrene central core that is rather protected. That the latter is indeed the case is established based on quenching
## Chemical Science
studies with a model compound, i.e., PTE, whose fluorescence is quenched by other amino acids as well. Further, the selectivity also establishes the fact that the quenching observed with histidine cannot be a surface phenomenon, for the quenching should otherwise be observed for amino acids such as tryptophan and tyrosine.
Histidine is one of the three basic amino acids, which exist as cationic species in the aqueous medium.19 We suspected that the anionic Zn-PLA with DMA species as counter cations presumably undergoes cation exchange with these amino acids when suspended in their aqueous solutions. Independently, the ability of Zn-PLA crystals to undergo postsynthetic exchange (PSE)20 of DMA cations was investigated by suspending the MOF crystals in the solution of a cationic dye, namely, methylene blue. Indeed, the crystals of Zn-PLA were found to adsorb the cationic dye, i.e., methylene blue, selectively without touching the anionic bromophenol blue and neutral nile red dye, see Figure S11, ESI; blue color of the dye permits dye exchange to be made out by naked eye via blue coloration of the crystals. This suggests that Zn-PLA may undergo postsynthetic cation exchange with all the three basic amino acids that become cationic in the aqueous medium, leading to transport of these amino acids into the interiors of the crystals of Zn-PLA. Evidently, this should not be feasible for all other amino acids. Why is it then that the fluorescence of Zn-PLA is influenced only by histidine and not by the other two basic amino acids, i.e., arginine and lysine?
## 3. Conclusions
A chiral organic linker $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ with inherent concave shapes for guest inclusion was rationally designed to access porous MOFs by metal-assisted self assembly. Treatment $\mathbf{H_{4}P L A}$ with $Z_{\mathrm{{n}}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{3})_{2}$ led to porous and luminescent crystals of Zn-PLA, for which the solid-state fluorescence quantum yield is $46\%$ . The fluorescence of aqueous dispersion of Zn-PLA was found to be quenched specifically by histidine amongst all other amino acids, leading to its selective sensing. Given that the fluorescence of the model organic compound that is devoid of carboxy groups is quenched by amino acids such as cysteine, histidine, tryptophan and tyrosine with diffusion limited rates, the selective quenching of fluorescence of Zn-PLA by histidine is remarkable. The reason for the latter has been rationalized based on exchange of the cationic DMA species in the MOF crystals by histidine that is protonated in water. The cationic imidazolium ring of histidine is proposed to involve in charge-transfer interactions with the protected pyrene fluorophore in its excited state for the observed quenching. The chirality extant to the MOF−imparted by the lactic acid moieties−allows enantiodiscrimination of D and L forms. The ratio of enantioselectivity, i.e., $K_{\mathrm{D}}/K_{\mathrm{L}},$ is determined to be 1.8 from Stern-Volmer quenching plots. These results constitute first examples of highly selective and
## Chemical Science
enantiodifferentiating sensing of an amino acid, namely histidine in the present instance, by MOFs in water.
